# Logs
# Job logs are huge and disposable -- EXCEPT the result payloads written under any
# */results/ directory, which are real measurements and must be versioned like every
# other eval result. Re-including them needs all three lines: git will not look inside
# an excluded directory, so the directories have to be un-ignored before the files in
# them can be.
logs/**
!logs/**/
!logs/**/results/**
qad/checkpoints/

# wandb local run state (synced to the server; nothing here needs versioning)
wandb/
qad/wandb/

# Eval results are versioned -- ALL of them, every tree. There was an ignore rule here
# for qad/eval_results_disagg_think/ meant to keep a live sweep out of accidental
# commits; it outlived its purpose and became a trap, because it hides new result
# directories rather than new files in existing ones. The 0.6B results were force-added
# and so stayed visible, but the whole 1.7B think sweep would have landed untracked and
# silently absent from the plots.
attic_invalid_kvd/
qad/cluster_scripts/.eval_submissions.jsonl

# arm_comparison.json is DERIVED, not measured, and is the one exception to the rule
# above. compare_arms.py writes it and nothing -- no driver, no notebook -- ever reads it
# back; the `signflip` rows it appears to consult come from the in-memory return value in
# the same run. It regenerates from the versioned scores/ export in seconds, and its
# `benches` field records whichever --benches the last run happened to pass, so a
# committed copy is not canonical: the one in this tree said ['mmlu_pro'] alone.
#
# Versioning it meant that merely READING the tables dirtied the tree, and that two
# branches which had both looked at the results collided on a file neither had measured.
# The measurements themselves -- summary.json and evals/scores/ -- stay versioned.
evals/results/*/arm_comparison.json

qad/data/
qad/cluster_scripts/bench_*.py

# Eval generations. Logged by default (see bin/run_eval*.sh) because the scores alone
# cannot answer later questions -- length, refusals, format failures, repetition loops --
# and re-running a sweep to recover them costs far more than the disk. They are NOT
# versioned: ~1 MB per (step, task, mode, format, model) would add gigabytes. The scores
# in step_<N>.json are the committed artifact; these sit beside them on lustre.
qad/results/**/*_samples_*.jsonl

# lloyd43 kernel package: build + test artifacts
qad/kernels/lloyd43/.pytest_cache/
qad/kernels/lloyd43/src/*.egg-info/
qad/kernels/lloyd43/build/

# Eval generations. Same rule as qad/results: the SCORES are versioned (summary.json
# and the scorer outputs beside it), the raw generations are not -- MMMU-Pro vision
# alone is 1730 responses x a multi-thousand-token reasoning chain.
#
# These patterns said `muse/` until the tree was renamed to `evals/`, at which point
# they silently stopped matching and 88 MB of generations across 92 files went into the
# first commit after the rename. A gitignore rule keyed on a directory name is a rule
# that breaks the moment the directory is renamed, and it breaks SILENTLY -- nothing
# fails, the commit just gets bigger.
evals/results/**/raw.jsonl
# raw.shard0of4.jsonl and friends: the ** pattern above matches the exact
# filename only, so sharded runs would have been committed by accident.
evals/results/**/raw.shard*.jsonl
evals/results/**/rep*.jsonl
evals/results/**/responses.jsonl

# scored.jsonl -- ocrbench's per-item scores, 8 MB across 18 arms and growing with every
# arm. It is DERIVED from raw.jsonl, which is ignored, and every number in it is now in
# evals/scores/ in a form that is a tenth the size, covers all five benchmarks rather
# than one, and is what compare_arms reads off-cluster. Versioning both would put the
# same measurement in history twice, in two formats, with no way to tell which was
# authoritative when they disagree.
evals/results/**/scored.jsonl

# generation_complete -- a marker holding one integer, written so the ocrbench scorer can
# tell a finished arm from a resumed one mid-flight. It is state for the pipeline, not a
# measurement, and the same count is in summary.json's n_scored.
evals/results/**/generation_complete

# RULER's generated haystacks are INPUTS, not measurements, and are the one thing under
# evals/ that is not versioned. They are regenerated deterministically by RULER's own
# scripts/data/prepare.py under a fixed seed (--random_seed 42) from the corpora in
# harnesses/RULER, so nothing is lost by omitting them. Size is the reason: the 4k
# corpus alone is 81 MB for 13 tasks x 500 docs, and cost scales with length -- the 64k
# leg is ~16x that, putting a full 5-length sweep in the gigabytes.
#
# Scoped to the corpus directory ONLY. evals/results/**, including every RULER
# summary.json and raw.jsonl, stays versioned like all other measurements.
evals/data/ruler/

01_BRAND_ASSETS/

# Local assistant workflows, scheduler logs and schematic previews
/.claude/
slurm-*.out
/notebooks/schematics/preview*.png
